AN ORDERED LIST INVENTS A SECOND NUMBERED STEP.

WHAT md DOES TODAY:

    1. Ordered parent

        - Nested note

    1. Tail paragraph of the parent.     <-- invented step

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. The tail is a continuation paragraph of step 1, so it is
written at the content indent of that step, which for a `1.` marker is three
columns, and it carries no number.

WHY THIS IS WORSE ON AN ORDERED LIST THAN ON A BULLET ONE. A bullet list that
gains an item gains a bullet. An ordered list that gains an item gains a
STEP: the document now describes a two step procedure where the author wrote
one step and a note about it. Prose that explains a step is turned into an
instruction to be carried out. A reader following the list does an extra thing
that nobody asked for.

The same defect as `format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item`, seen on an
ordered list. This case exists separately because the harm is different in
kind, and because the continuation indent differs: three columns after `1. `
rather than two after `- `. A fix that hard-codes the bullet width would pass
that case and still fail this one.

CAUSE and a fix that fits the flat model: see the marker on
`format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item`.
